From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude (agent session)" <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 19:54:50 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] font: DirectWrite/D2D bindings, validated against the live
 system

Foundation for the directwrite_harfbuzz backend (ADR 0005). Not yet referenced
by the build - Zig does not compile unreachable files, so this cannot break
anything; the Face commit that follows makes them reachable and is where
zig build test starts covering them.

Every interface here was validated empirically by spikes before being
committed, because a COM vtable slot declared at the wrong offset silently
calls the wrong function rather than failing to compile:

  DirectWrite    194 font families enumerated; Consolas upem=2048 ascent=1884;
                 glyph('A')=0x4; Consolas has no U+1F600 (notdef) while Segoe
                 UI Emoji maps it to 0x8DA; COLR table reported as 7133988
                 bytes version 1 - matching an independent binary parse of the
                 font file exactly
  D2D + WIC      rasterized 'A' at 32px into a WIC bitmap and read it back:
                 22x42 cell, stride 88, recognisable glyph with grayscale AA
  Colour glyphs  U+1F600 through TranslateColorGlyphRun: 6 layers, 55 distinct
                 opaque colours

Two design notes, both driven by reading what AtlasEngine actually ships.

Rasterization goes through D2D rather than IDWriteGlyphRunAnalysis. AtlasEngine
has both and its GlyphRunAnalysis path is behind #if 0, with the note that
Direct2D is "roughly 2x faster". More importantly TranslateColorGlyphRun
returning DWRITE_E_NOCOLOR *is* the "this glyph is monochrome" test, so one
rasterizer covers both cases instead of two plus a rule for choosing.

TranslateColorGlyphRun is taken from IDWriteFactory2, not Factory4. Factory4
additionally handles SVG/PNG/bitmap glyph formats but sits ~43 vtable slots
deep through two more inheritance levels, each hand-counted offset failing
silently if wrong. Factory2 handles COLR, which is what Segoe UI Emoji uses
and exactly what AtlasEngine renders - it requests COLR on an IDWriteFactory4
and never asks for paint trees. Factory4 is a later upgrade for rarer formats.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
